      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams execute them badly, but because of what they are asked to be. A roadmap is simultaneously expected to serve as a strategy, a commitment, a communication tool, and a negotiation settlement. These purposes conflict, and the document that results usually satisfies none of them.\n\n**They confuse output with outcome.** The typical roadmap is a list of features arranged against dates. It answers *what will we build* while leaving *what will change as a result* unstated. This makes success unfalsifiable in the only way that matters: a team can ship every item on schedule and move no metric, satisfy no customer, and win no market. Because the artifact measures delivery, delivery becomes the goal, and the roadmap quietly converts a company's ambition into a shipping schedule.\n\n**They encode false confidence.** Uncertainty compounds with time. What a team knows about the next six weeks is qualitatively different from what it knows about month nine, yet most roadmaps render both in the same crisp rectangles. Once a date appears in a slide, it hardens into a promise — to sales, to executives, to customers — and the team spends the following quarters defending a forecast made when it knew the least. Learning something inconvenient becomes a political problem rather than a welcome result.\n\n**They are political settlements, not strategies.** Roadmaps often emerge from a process of appeasement: each stakeholder's priority gets a slot, and peace is purchased with inclusion. The output is a list that is technically comprehensive and strategically incoherent — no theory of why *these* bets, in *this* order, compound into advantage. Strategy requires explicit refusal, and most roadmaps contain no visible \"no.\" Where nothing has been declined, nothing has been chosen.\n\n**They ignore the physics of the organization.** Roadmaps are usually built at one hundred percent of theoretical capacity, with no allowance for maintenance, incidents, support escalations, hiring gaps, or the discovery work that precedes real building. Any of these — and all of them are certain — turns the plan into a debt schedule. The first slip cascades, and by mid-year the roadmap survives only as a document nobody consults.\n\n**They lack a feedback loop.** A plan that cannot be revised is not a plan but a prophecy. Many organizations have no ritual for killing an item, no defined evidence that would justify a change of course, and no owner empowered to make one. Sunk cost and reputational commitment keep dead initiatives alive long past their expiry.\n\nWhat survives, by contrast, looks different in kind rather than degree. It states the problems worth solving and the outcomes that would prove progress. It expresses time as confidence — now, next, later — rather than as false precision. It is short enough to be strategic and specific enough to be actionable, and it is expected to change as the team learns, which is the entire point of building anything.\n\nA roadmap should be a hypothesis about how a company will win. Most are merely an inventory of what it has promised.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nWhy Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nThe roadmap was supposed to bring clarity. Instead, three months later no one trusts it, no one follows it, and everyone quietly builds something else. This is the norm, not the exception. Most product roadmaps fail — and it’s rarely because teams aren’t working hard.\n\nFirst, we confuse a roadmap with a release plan. A classic roadmap is a timeline packed with features and dates: “Mobile checkout in Q2, AI search in Q3.” It looks decisive, but it promises certainty we don’t have. When discovery takes longer, dependencies slip, or customers want something else, the entire plan collapses. Leaders see missed dates as failure, engineers feel set up, and trust evaporates.\n\nSecond, most roadmaps measure output, not outcome. They list what we will ship, not why it matters or what will change for the user and business. Without a clear problem to solve and metric to move, there’s no way to say no, to prioritize, or to know if we succeeded. Success becomes “we shipped on time,” even if no one uses it. That turns product teams into feature factories.\n\nThird, there is no strategy underneath. A good roadmap is a story about how we win: who we serve, what pain we uniquely solve, what we will not do. Too many roadmaps skip that and become a negotiated list of stakeholder requests. Sales wants this enterprise feature, Support wants that fix, the CEO saw a competitor launch something shiny. When a roadmap tries to please everyone, it serves no one. It’s political compromise disguised as a plan.\n\nFourth, roadmaps are treated as static contracts in a dynamic world. Markets shift, data arrives, assumptions prove wrong. But updating the roadmap feels like admitting failure, so teams either cling to an outdated plan or abandon it entirely. Both kill credibility.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps are never truly communicated. They live in a slide deck, full of jargon, with no shared understanding of trade-offs. Engineering doesn’t believe the dates, design wasn’t involved early, customers were never consulted.\n\nThe roadmaps that work do the opposite: they anchor on outcomes, embrace uncertainty, make hard choices explicit, and are revisited continuously. They are not promises of features. They are bets on how to create value — and like all bets, they are meant to be learned from, not just delivered.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
